{%- set faqs = [
  ["What is AI agent security testing?", "AI agent security testing is a structured assessment of an autonomous AI system that can plan, call tools and take actions on a user's behalf. Testers try to manipulate the agent, push it beyond its intended permissions and abuse the tools and credentials it holds. The result is a prioritized list of weaknesses, each with evidence and a recommended fix."],
  ["How is an AI agent different from a chatbot for security purposes?", "A chatbot produces text. An agent acts: it can send email, query databases, change records, run code or spend money. A successful attack on a chatbot usually exposes information, while a successful attack on an agent can cause a real action. That is why agents need their own testing, centered on permissions, tools and approval controls."],
  ["What is excessive agency?", "Excessive agency is a weakness in which an AI system is given more tools, permissions or autonomy than its task requires. When such an agent is manipulated, the damage is limited only by what it is allowed to do. Reducing agency to the minimum the task needs is one of the most effective ways to reduce risk."],
  ["What is indirect prompt injection against an agent?", "Indirect prompt injection hides instructions in content an agent reads while doing its job, such as a web page, an email, a support ticket or a shared document. The agent may treat those instructions as legitimate and act on them. Because agents read a great deal of untrusted content, this is among the most important attack paths to test."],
  ["What is least privilege for AI agents?", "Least privilege means the agent can reach only the tools, data and actions that its current task needs, using credentials scoped to that purpose. Testing checks whether the agent holds broader access than necessary, whether permissions are enforced outside the prompt, and what an attacker could do with the access the agent already has."],
  ["Do you test agents built on the Model Context Protocol (MCP)?", "Yes. Agents that connect to tools and data through MCP servers, plugins or custom integrations can be assessed. Testing covers how tools are described and authorized, whether tool output can carry hostile instructions, and how credentials are stored and passed."],
  ["Can you test multi-agent systems?", "Yes. When several agents delegate to each other, a weakness in one can spread to the rest. We test how agents trust each other's messages, whether one compromised agent can instruct another, and whether shared memory or context can be poisoned."],
  ["Which AI agents can you test?", "We can test customer service agents, coding and developer agents, browser and computer-use agents, workflow automation agents, data analysis agents and multi-agent systems. Agents built with commercial platforms, open-source frameworks or custom code can all be assessed."],
  ["Will testing cause the agent to take real actions?", "Testing is planned to avoid harm. We prefer a staging environment, test accounts and sandboxed tools, and we agree in advance which actions may be exercised for real. Where an action would be destructive, we verify that the attack path exists without completing it."],
  ["Do you review human-in-the-loop approvals?", "Yes. Many agents rely on a person to approve sensitive actions. We test whether those approvals can be bypassed, whether the approval request shows the real action, and whether an attacker could overwhelm or mislead the reviewer."],
  ["What does an AI agent security report include?", "The report includes an executive summary, an inventory of the agent's tools and permissions, each finding with its severity and steps to reproduce, supporting evidence, the affected component and a recommended fix. Findings can be mapped to the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications and to agentic AI threat guidance."],
  ["How long does AI agent security testing take?", "Duration depends on the number of agents, the tools and systems they can reach, and the depth of testing required. A single-purpose agent can be assessed in a short engagement, while multi-agent systems with many integrations take longer. We confirm the timeline after scoping."],
  ["How much does AI agent security testing cost?", "Cost depends on the number of agents and tools, the complexity of the integrations, the testing depth and the reporting scope. Share a short description of your agent with CipherTrivia and we will recommend a scope and quote."],
  ["When should we test an AI agent?", "Test before an agent is given access to production systems or customer data, after you add tools or widen permissions, and on a regular schedule once it is live. Each new tool or permission changes what a successful attack can do."]
] %}

